{
  "model": "base.en",
  "device": "cpu/int8",
  "language": "en",
  "text": "A VCPU count does not tell you how much sustained compute a VPS plan allows. Before buying a shared CPU server, check what the provider means by shared and what its written usage policy permits. Start with a workload. A small site with occasional bursts has different needs from a task that keeps the CPU busy continuously. Do not assume that a quiet test represents the way your service will behave all day. Next, read the documented CPU limits. Look for conditions for continuous CPU use, burst rules, and the provider's response when a workload exceeds them. If the policy is unclear, ask support a concrete question about your intended task before paying. Then, separate the plan label from a performance claim. Shared resources, dedicated resources, and the number of advertised virtual CPU's are different pieces of information. None of them alone proves your application's response time. Our take, buying a suitable resource policy matters more than collecting the largest headline CPU number. Keep a small comparison sheet with the plan, policy source, workload, and unanswered question. This is a purchasing checklist, not a benchmark. We have not run a performance test for this video and do not rank providers from an untested claim. The description links to the Fair Use Guide at vpsdealswire.com. Use the site to narrow your short list, then confirm the actual plan terms on the provider's official page.",
